WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.530
Phillip: Good communication in the workplace
should be effective, but there are often barriers

00:00:05.530 --> 00:00:10.139
that can affect this, so let’s have a look at
what they are and how we can overcome them.

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:16.729
Maddy: Cultural differences-These often
occur when the kind of language being used,

00:00:16.969 --> 00:00:22.349
such as the use of vocabulary or style, is
unsuitable or unfamiliar to the receiver.

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:26.420
This might be because the two people
involved are used to different workplace

00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:31.990
cultures, so may find one another’s style
of communicating unfamiliar and challenging.

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:37.990
Phillip: The important thing about overcoming
language barriers is to establish a common ground

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:40.300
that both sender and receiver are familiar with.

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:43.640
This will make communication
smoother and more reliable.

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:48.719
It's also important to bear in mind who
the audience is and what you can do to make

00:00:48.719 --> 00:00:50.410
the language more appropriate for them?

00:00:52.549 --> 00:00:56.010
Maddy: Inattention and a lack of engagement-
This may mean that neither the sender, the

00:00:56.010 --> 00:01:01.930
receiver, or both really care about communicating,
so not enough attention is being paid.

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:07.310
Communication doesn’t work if the people involved
don’t care about what’s being communicated.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:12.090
If they don’t care, they won’t make
the effort to communicate properly, or

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:13.810
care about the message they receive.

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:19.120
Phillip: It's important that the sender
invests time and energy into making the

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:23.970
communication relevant to the receiver
making it clear why it is important for them.

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:29.960
In turn receivers should try to avoid distractions
when they receive a message, so they can focus on

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:32.789
it fully and ask follow-up questions if needed.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:39.569
Maddy: Information overload- This can involve
too much information being communicated,

00:01:40.179 --> 00:01:43.480
and this could either be because the
wrong channel is being used, or the

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:45.550
information hasn't been coded correctly.

00:01:46.459 --> 00:01:51.660
If we try to communicate too much information
in one go, it won’t work because the

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:56.160
person receiving may feel overwhelmed,
with all the facts being thrown at them.

00:01:56.920 --> 00:02:00.249
Some methods are more flexible
with how much information they can

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:02.360
effectively transmit, than others.

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:07.479
Phillip: Senders need to be mindful of how
much information they need to communicate

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:09.709
and how much they are communicating.

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:14.420
It's easy to go into too much detail
about irrelevant points sometimes.

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:19.420
So remember in general, the shorter the
message, the easier it is to understand

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:24.679
[though that doesn't mean you should omit
important details if they're necessary].

00:02:25.339 --> 00:02:30.120
Maddy: Distractions and noise- These can be
literal, such as if you're trying to have a

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:35.600
sensitive conversation in a noisy business
area, or it can be more general, like if an

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:37.959
email is cluttered with unnecessary pictures.

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:42.950
Distractions and noise pull the ‘receiver’
away from the point of the communication,

00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:48.330
meaning they might walk away not realising or
understanding what the point was meant to be.

00:02:49.890 --> 00:02:52.750
Phillip: Keep communications
clear and to the point.

00:02:53.389 --> 00:02:58.420
Don't throw in visuals or graphics or
fancy phrases just for the sake of them.

00:02:59.059 --> 00:03:02.300
Everything in the message should
have a reason to be there.

00:03:02.630 --> 00:03:06.929
And remember to make sure that conversations
happen somewhere quiet and private,

00:03:07.130 --> 00:03:09.840
especially if they're on a sensitive subject.

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:16.430
Maddy: Emotions- In nonverbal and oral
communication, it’s easy to let our emotions

00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:19.080
get the better of us and undermine our message.

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:23.990
Although there’s often a place for some
emotional expression in communication, it can

00:03:23.990 --> 00:03:26.790
also sometimes inhibit effective communication.

00:03:27.530 --> 00:03:32.340
That’s because the way we’re feeling, can
often cloud our rational mind, meaning we’re

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:34.689
not receptive to what’s actually being said.

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:38.660
We might feel that something unfair
has been said, meaning we don’t

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:40.460
want to listen to valid criticism.

00:03:41.090 --> 00:03:45.509
Or we might feel so frustrated about
a situation that our communications

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:47.480
sound angry and aggressive.

00:03:48.389 --> 00:03:52.670
Phillip: It’s important to remember human beings
aren't robots and there's nothing wrong with

00:03:52.670 --> 00:03:54.880
your emotions coming through in communication!

00:03:55.450 --> 00:03:59.670
But you need to consider how you're
expressing your feelings, and if it's

00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:01.470
appropriate to do so at that time.

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:06.649
Being too emotive or trying to provoke
an emotional response in the receiver,

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:08.940
might not always be appropriate.

00:04:10.660 --> 00:04:15.549
Maddy: Lack of clarity- This could be
due to poor encoding or an inappropriate

00:04:15.550 --> 00:04:20.700
channel, the information in the message
just isn't clear to the receiver or isn’t

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:23.100
interpreted in the way you meant it to be.

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:26.930
If this happens then this
is a failed communication.

00:04:27.670 --> 00:04:31.679
Phillip: If a communication is too
vague or too long, the receiver may

00:04:31.690 --> 00:04:33.870
feel like they're time is being wasted.

00:04:34.570 --> 00:04:37.520
They'll lose focus and miss important information.

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:41.579
Your communications should
be direct and to the point.

00:04:42.230 --> 00:04:47.770
Avoid using complicated imagery or metaphors and
be clear about the purpose of the communication.

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:52.550
Maddy: Wrong style and tone -The message
could be using the right channel,

00:04:52.930 --> 00:04:58.520
but is too formal and impersonal, or
the opposite too informal and casual.

00:04:59.090 --> 00:05:03.089
This means that the receiver won’t be as
receptive to the message because it won’t

00:05:03.089 --> 00:05:07.859
feel right to them and will undermine the
communication, making it less effective.

00:05:08.779 --> 00:05:12.070
Phillip: Everyone has their own style of
writing and speaking, and that's fine.

00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:17.030
In fact, a bit of personality can often make
your communication easier to understand.

00:05:17.660 --> 00:05:23.000
But be mindful of your tone and style and
think about what your audience is expecting

00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:24.800
from you, and what you need to say.

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:28.950
Think about some of the communications
you have received in the past and how

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:30.420
you might have said things differently.

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:36.780
Maddy: So, there are a range of barriers to
communication in the workplace, but these barriers

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:42.239
can be overcome if you just take some time
to consider how they might be received and if

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:44.869
you could communicate in a more effective way.

